Jahshaka patch 0071 — THE SECOND Xid 109 SITE: A LARGE 3D STORAGE IMAGE
CREATED AND DESTROYED EVERY VOXELISER BUILD LOSES THE DEVICE.

SOURCE patch (one file: `Components/Hlms/Pbs/src/Vct/OgreVctVoxelizer.cpp`).
EVERY TREE MUST RE-RUN `irisgl/scripts/build-ogre.sh` after pulling it. It shares
that file with patches 0061/0062/0064/0065 (it amends 0065's own lines), so a
tree that carries those and fails the script's per-patch reverse check is seeing
the documented overlap false positive; the cure is the recorded reset recipe.

THE SYMPTOM (Jahshaka lane XID-2, 2026-09-17, on push #41's stack 0001-0069,
NVIDIA 595.84, RTX 4080 SUPER). THE OWNER, in a very simple scene: a gold cube
and two spheres on the default ground at Epic, a large plane laid over the floor,
then the plane HIDDEN in the outliner — the app froze, `vkWaitForFences
VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST in VulkanQueue::_waitOnFrame`, "Vulkan: Device was lost,
recreating", and a kernel `NVRM: Xid (PCI:0000:01:00): 109, pid=<that app>,
name=Jahshaka, CTX SWITCH TIMEOUT`. Patch 0067 (the delayed-block reuse window)
was already in that build: this is a SECOND, independent site.

WHAT IT IS. `VctVoxelizer::build()` ends by scheduling the voxel scratch volumes
`OnStorage` and `createVoxelTextures()` brings them back `Resident` at the start
of the next build. For upstream's `mAccumValVox` that is one small volume; patch
0065 gave the same treatment to the order-independent merge accumulator, which is
THIRTEEN texels per voxel (`mDepth * 13u`, R32_UINT) — 13.6 MB at 64^3 and 109 MB
at 128^3, the largest single allocation the engine makes per build. Under a
Photon cascade chain a build happens every frame or two, so the round trip runs
continuously: a large 3D storage image is destroyed and a new one created while
the compute dispatches that wrote the previous one may still be executing. On
this driver that hangs the channel.

THE MEASUREMENTS THAT LOCATE IT (the owner's sequence scripted — a new Epic
project, a cube and two spheres, a 40x plane, then hide/show every 20 frames for
100 cycles; a cold data root every run; the kernel log followed for the whole
session and every app pid matched against it; a run is a HANG when the app stops
printing and never returns):

    arm                                                      hangs / runs   Xid
    the owner's sequence, unchanged                              4 / 4      4
    ... ray tracing off (world.rayTracing 'off')                 6 / 6      6
    ... the 512 MB force-flush disabled entirely                 6 / 6      6
    ... delayed blocks held 16 frames instead of 3               4 / 6      4
    ... both of the above together                               3 / 6      3
    ... every cached VkImageView purged on residency loss        6 / 6      6
    ... the merge accumulator kept RESIDENT (THIS PATCH)         0 / 6      0
    ... it and mAccumValVox both kept resident                   0 / 6      0

So it is not the ray-query tier, not the force-flush path, not the cached image
views, and not the window in which freed memory is handed back — no amount of
delaying the MEMORY changes it. Keeping the IMAGE alive removes it completely.
(The same arm was measured by lane XID-1 before patch 0067 existed, 0/8; 0067
fixed a real and separate upstream defect and left this one standing.)

THE FIX. The merge accumulator is created once with the voxel textures and stays
Resident until they are destroyed. `destroyVoxelTextures()` already destroys it,
so nothing leaks and a resolution change still re-creates it. Upstream's
`mAccumValVox` keeps its own transient treatment: the measurement says it is not
the trigger, and changing upstream behaviour without evidence is not this lane's
business.

THE COST: one accumulator per voxeliser held for that voxeliser's lifetime —
width * height * depth * 13 * 4 bytes, i.e. 13.6 MB at 64^3 (a Photon cascade in
a default scene) and 109 MB at 128^3 (the single-volume arm at the top quality).
Under a chain that memory was never actually free anyway — the next build asked
for it again a frame later. Sharing ONE scratch volume across a chain's cascades
would reduce it further and belongs to a Photon lane, not to a device-loss fix.

UPSTREAM-REPORTABLE, and the entry belongs in SPECS/OGRE_UPSTREAM_ISSUES.md: the
transient-scratch pattern (`scheduleTransitionTo( OnStorage )` at the end of a
build, `Resident` at the start of the next) is upstream's own, and on this driver
it is only survivable while the volume is small. Anything that uses it for a
large 3D UAV should expect the same hang.

VERIFICATION. `git apply --check` on a tree carrying 0001-0070 and
`git apply --reverse --check` after applying; `build-ogre.sh` re-run in the lane
worktree. The reproducer 16/16 with ZERO Xid in the followed kernel log;
`--engine-selftest` unchanged (this patch moves no pixels — the accumulator is a
scratch volume, cleared at the start of every build either way); the gi suites
that own the voxeliser green.

diff --git a/Components/Hlms/Pbs/src/Vct/OgreVctVoxelizer.cpp b/Components/Hlms/Pbs/src/Vct/OgreVctVoxelizer.cpp
--- a/Components/Hlms/Pbs/src/Vct/OgreVctVoxelizer.cpp
+++ b/Components/Hlms/Pbs/src/Vct/OgreVctVoxelizer.cpp
@@ -858,8 +858,9 @@
             mAlbedoVox->getDepth() == mDepth )
         {
             mAccumValVox->scheduleTransitionTo( GpuResidency::Resident );
-            // Jahshaka patch 0065: the merge accumulator is transient too.
-            mMergeAccumTex->scheduleTransitionTo( GpuResidency::Resident );
+            // Jahshaka patch 0071: the merge accumulator is NOT transient. It is
+            // created once with the voxel textures and stays Resident until they
+            // are destroyed -- see the note at the end of build().
             return;
         }
 
@@ -1604,7 +1605,29 @@
         // These textures are no longer needed, they're not used for the injection
         // phase. Save memory.
         mAccumValVox->scheduleTransitionTo( GpuResidency::OnStorage );
-        mMergeAccumTex->scheduleTransitionTo( GpuResidency::OnStorage );
+
+        // THE MERGE ACCUMULATOR STAYS RESIDENT (Jahshaka patch 0071). Patch 0065
+        // gave it upstream's transient treatment: OnStorage at the end of every
+        // build(), Resident at the start of the next one. On NVIDIA 595.84 that
+        // per-build create/destroy of a large 3D storage image, while the
+        // dispatches that wrote the previous one may still be executing, HANGS
+        // THE CHANNEL: NVRM Xid 109 CTX SWITCH TIMEOUT -> VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST.
+        // Measured (lane XID-2, the owner's own sequence scripted -- hide and
+        // show a plane in an Epic scene, 100 cycles a run): 4/4 and 6/6 runs
+        // lost the device with the round trip, 0/6 and 0/6 without it, every
+        // failure carrying a kernel Xid line from that pid and no passing run
+        // ever carrying one. It is NOT the delayed-block reuse window (patch
+        // 0067's subject: a 16-frame window still hangs 4/6), NOT the 512 MB
+        // force-flush (disabled: 6/6), NOT the cached image views (purged on
